task: dataflow-durable
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-durable
worktree: ../okay-wt-ddu1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stages 9 and 10

why: the last two open dataflow entries are the same question asked
  twice — what a real STORE buys that a cell cannot.

  dataflow-fenced-commit: stage 10's fence is a check before a write,
  so a leader deposed between the two can land one stale commit.
  dataflow-durable-stage: stage 9's staging sink keeps its epoch in
  memory, so the two-phase commit survives the coordinator's death
  and not a half-applied move.

what: close both by the same observation — a JOURNAL THAT KEEPS ITS
  HISTORY CAN DEFEND ITSELF, where a single cell cannot.

  1. the term goes INTO the record. A resume then takes the highest
     (term, epoch) rather than the last thing written, so a stale
     commit from a deposed leader is shadowed for ever instead of
     being read back. That closes the window on the READ side without
     a compare-and-set, which no store here offers.
  2. the writer's atomicity is the writer's, and the contract says so
     rather than implying it. A `move` that records the epoch in ONE
     write with its rows cannot half-apply; a log append is exactly
     that, and the test writes one record per epoch to prove the
     shape rather than describing it.

what this is NOT: a compare-and-set. The check stays, because it stops
  a ghost cheaply and at the right moment; what is added is that being
  wrong about it no longer costs correctness.

predict: (1) is small and the test is the work — a ghost has to get
  PAST the check for the read-side defence to be what saves the run,
  so the test must make the lease lie rather than merely expire. (2)
  may turn out to need no code at all, in which case the entry closes
  as a contract made explicit and a demonstration, and I will say that
  rather than invent a mechanism to justify the lane.
